@Tutorial(time: 25) {
    @Intro(title: "Testing Reducers") {
        This is the Reducer-pattern counterpart to the classic Testing tutorial. We'll build a small
        news feed feature — a parent `NewsFeedReducer` with a child `NewsFeedArticleReducer` — and write
        the full range of acceptance tests against it: basic flow, effects, child-store navigation, and
        dependency injection. Every state assertion in this tutorial reads through `.state.`, since a
        Reducer's data always lives one level down from the `Store` itself.
    }

    @Section(title: "Basic flow testing") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Before writing tests, we need something to test. We'll define two reducers — a child that
            loads and favorites a single article, and a parent that lists articles and navigates to one
            of them — then write the first acceptance tests against the parent.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Start with the supporting types: a `DateProvider` and a `PersistenceProvider` as
                injectable dependencies, plus the plain data types the feature works with.

                @Code(name: "Tutorial06_Testing_Reducer.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-Dependencies-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Define the child reducer. `NewsFeedArticleReducer` loads its own title via an effect and
                lets the user toggle it as a favorite. Nothing here differs from a top-level reducer —
                a child is just a `Reducer` that happens to be wired under a parent's `@SubState`.

                @Code(name: "Tutorial06_Testing_Reducer.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-ChildReducer-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Define the parent reducer. `readingArticle` is declared `@SubState`, so assigning it
                creates the child `Store` automatically. `favorite(id:)` resolves both dependencies
                through `ReducerDependencies` — no `@Injected` property wrapper needed on the reducer
                itself, since reducers are stateless structs.

                @Code(name: "Tutorial06_Testing_Reducer.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-ParentReducer-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Write the first test: GIVEN an initial state (with mocked dependencies injected),
                THEN assert the starting values, WHEN an event is sent, THEN assert the result.
                `runTest(assertNoPendingEffects: false)` is needed here because `.systemLoadedScope`
                enqueues an effect this test never completes.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-BasicFlow-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Complete the loop by resolving that effect. `WHEN_OlderEffectCompletes` simulates the
                effect finishing with a given result, exactly as it would after a real network call.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-EffectCompletion-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                A longer test can walk through several state transitions in sequence, asserting after
                each one — useful for features (like favoriting) where the interesting behavior is in
                how repeated events compose, not any single one.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-StateTransitions-01.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Testing scope composition") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            `readingArticle` is a child `Store`, not just a value — so beyond asserting on it, we can
            navigate into it and run the same GIVEN/WHEN/THEN vocabulary against the child directly.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Sending `.navigateToChild` assigns `state.readingArticle`, which wires a new
                `Store<NewsFeedArticleReducer>`. `store.children.readingArticle` exposes the live child.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-Navigation-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                `.WITH(\.children.readingArticle)` descends into the child store — every step after it
                runs against `Store<NewsFeedArticleReducer>` instead of the parent, including its own
                effect completions. `.POP()` returns to the parent to keep asserting there.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-WITH-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                For quick one-off assertions or sends, a keypath through `.children.<name>?.state.*`
                reaches into the child without a full `WITH`/`POP` pair. The keypath shape is the one
                real difference from the classic pattern — Reducer inserts `.children.` and `.state.`
                where Statostore just chains straight through the `@Subscope` property.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-Keypaths-01.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Dependencies and custom assertions") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Not every assertion fits `.THEN(keyPath:, equals:)`. Closures let you assert on multiple
            properties at once, verify side effects on injected fakes, or express the acceptance
            criteria for a whole user-facing feature as a single readable test.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                `.THEN { store in ... }` hands you the live store for custom `XCTAssert` calls —
                useful when a single assertion needs to look at more than one property at a time.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-CustomAssertions-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Injected dependencies are ordinary fakes: closures that capture local state. Asserting
                that `persistedFavorites` was actually written to verifies the reducer called through to
                its injected `PersistenceProvider`, not just that its own state changed.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-DependencyInjection-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Put it together: a single test can read as the acceptance criteria for "user can save an
                article as a favorite" — load the feed, navigate to an article, favorite it, done. This
                is the payoff of naming `State` and `When` like sentences from the first tutorial onward.

                @Code(name: "TestingPatternsTests.swift", file: Testing-Reducer-Capstone-01.swift)
            }

            That's the full testing vocabulary for the Reducer pattern: `GIVEN`/`WHEN`/`THEN` for flow,
            `WHEN_OlderEffectCompletes` for effects, `WITH`/`POP` or `.children.`-keypaths for child
            stores, and closures or injected fakes for anything more specific. All of it runs against
            production reducer code — there's nothing test-only about the reducers themselves.
        }
    }
}
